From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2_codec2: drop reused output frames of a reallocated queue

After flush(), V4L2Decoder moves the frames still queued at the device
into mReuseFrameQueue and queues them again one posted task at a time.
If a source change reallocates the CAPTURE queue in between, for
example after a seek right after a flush into a part of the stream with
another bit depth, startOutputQueue() clears the queue and the block ID
map, but the tasks already posted still carry frames of the old queue.
They get V4L2 buffer IDs of the new queue and are queued with a buffer
allocated for the old format. Going from 8 to 10 bits at 1920x1088 the
NV12 dma-buf is smaller than the P010 plane, QBUF fails (videobuf2:
"failed to attach dmabuf") and the component goes to the error state.
In the other direction a P010 buffer would be filled as NV12.

Count the output queue allocations and drop a reused frame taken before
the latest one. Its block goes back to its pool, and the new queue is
filled from the pool created for it.

Checked on SC8280XP (Huawei MateBook E Go) with an HEVC stream that
switches from 10 to 8 bits after 120 frames, flushed and seeking back
into the 10-bit part 8 frames after the switch: QBUF failed in 2 of 6
runs before and passes in 12 of 12 with the patch, as does the opposite
switch. Decoding of 8-bit and 10-bit streams with seeks is unchanged.

Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp                        | 28 ++++++++++++++++++++-
 v4l2/include/v4l2_codec2/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.h |  6 +++++
 2 files changed, 33 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp b/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp
index 24e98e5..f2050cb 100644
--- a/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp
+++ b/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.cpp
@@ -350,6 +350,11 @@ bool V4L2Decoder::startOutputQueue(size_t minOutputBuffersCount, enum v4l2_memor
     while (!mReuseFrameQueue.empty()) {
         mReuseFrameQueue.pop();
     }
+    // A frame that tryFetchVideoFrame() has already taken from mReuseFrameQueue
+    // is still waiting in a posted onReuseFrameReady() task. Its buffer was
+    // allocated for the old format and size, so retire it together with the
+    // queue it belonged to.
+    mOutputQueueGeneration++;
 
     const size_t adjustedNumOutputBuffers =
             mOutputQueue->allocateBuffers(*numOutputBuffers, memory);
@@ -859,10 +864,31 @@ void V4L2Decoder::tryFetchVideoFrame() {
     mReuseFrameQueue.pop();
 
     // Avoid recursive calls
-    mTaskRunner->PostTask(FROM_HERE, ::base::BindOnce(&V4L2Decoder::onVideoFrameReady, mWeakThis,
+    mTaskRunner->PostTask(FROM_HERE, ::base::BindOnce(&V4L2Decoder::onReuseFrameReady, mWeakThis,
+                                                      mOutputQueueGeneration,
                                                       std::make_pair(std::move(frame), blockId)));
 }
 
+void V4L2Decoder::onReuseFrameReady(uint64_t generation,
+                                    VideoFramePool::FrameWithBlockId frameWithBlockId) {
+    ALOGV("%s(generation=%llu)", __func__, static_cast<unsigned long long>(generation));
+    ALOG_ASSERT(mTaskRunner->RunsTasksInCurrentSequence());
+
+    if (generation != mOutputQueueGeneration) {
+        // The output buffers were reallocated after this frame was taken from
+        // mReuseFrameQueue, e.g. by a resolution or bit depth change right
+        // after a flush. The block may be too small for the new buffers (QBUF
+        // fails) or have the wrong layout (wrong picture). Dropping the frame
+        // returns the block to its pool; changeResolution() has already
+        // started fetching blocks for the new buffers.
+        ALOGI("%s(): dropping blockId=%u of an old output queue", __func__,
+              frameWithBlockId.second);
+        return;
+    }
+
+    onVideoFrameReady(std::move(frameWithBlockId));
+}
+
 void V4L2Decoder::onVideoFrameReady(
         std::optional<VideoFramePool::FrameWithBlockId> frameWithBlockId) {
     ALOGV("%s()", __func__);
diff --git a/v4l2/include/v4l2_codec2/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.h b/v4l2/include/v4l2_codec2/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.h
index c825f74..5d0d204 100644
--- a/v4l2/include/v4l2_codec2/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.h
+++ b/v4l2/include/v4l2_codec2/v4l2/V4L2Decoder.h
@@ -92,6 +92,9 @@ private:
 
     void tryFetchVideoFrame();
     void onVideoFrameReady(std::optional<VideoFramePool::FrameWithBlockId> frameWithBlockId);
+    // Queues a frame taken from mReuseFrameQueue unless the output buffers
+    // have been reallocated since (|generation| is not the current one).
+    void onReuseFrameReady(uint64_t generation, VideoFramePool::FrameWithBlockId frameWithBlockId);
 
     std::optional<size_t> getNumOutputBuffers();
     std::optional<struct v4l2_format> getFormatInfo();
@@ -148,6 +151,9 @@ private:
     // is called. This is a workaround for b/297228544 and helps with general
     // responsiveness of the video playback due to b/270003218.
     std::queue<std::pair<size_t, std::unique_ptr<VideoFrame>>> mReuseFrameQueue;
+    // Counts the output buffer allocations done by startOutputQueue(). A frame
+    // taken from mReuseFrameQueue carries the value current at that time.
+    uint64_t mOutputQueueGeneration = 0;
 
     // Block IDs can be arbitrarily large, but we only have a limited number of
     // buffers. This maintains an association between a block ID and a specific
